    87	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    88	# Deterministic risk classification (the tier FLOOR, derived from the change
    89	# itself).
    90	#
    91	# Why this exists: every gate below keys off `risk_tier`, but that tier was
    92	# self-declared at `init`. A change mislabelled L1 silently skips the L3 gates —
    93	# no alarm, no evidence that anything was skipped. This is the one place the
    94	# whole chain could be defeated by a typo or by optimism. So the tier a human
    95	# (or an AI) declares is now only a FLOOR CANDIDATE: `classify` reads the paths
    96	# that actually changed and emits its own floor, and `gate --min-tier` takes the
    97	# MAX. Classification can therefore only ever RAISE the requirement.
    98	#
    99	# Rules are PATH-based on purpose: paths are cheap, stable, and reviewable, and
   100	# an attacker who renames a migration out of `migrations/` to dodge L3 has to do
   101	# something visible in the diff. Content sniffing was deliberately NOT used —
   102	# it is easy to defeat with formatting and produces false negatives that read as
   103	# safety. Deliberately blunt beats subtly wrong here.
   104	#
   105	# Ordering: first match wins per file, HIGHEST tier over all files wins overall.
   106	# Anything unrecognised is L1, never L0 — an unknown file is not evidence of
   107	# safety.
   108	# Two ways a keyword can end, and the difference is the whole false-positive story.
   109	#
   110	# _kw  — any casing, then a separator, a camelCase hump, or end of name. Used for
   111	#        the DANGEROUS words (auth, credentials, billing, worker...). AuthGuard.tsx
   112	#        and authGuard.tsx both reach L3, and a component that merely sounds like
   113	#        auth being reviewed as auth costs a review nobody regrets.
   114	# _kws — the same, EXCEPT that a PascalCase name does not count as a hump. Used for
   115	#        words that collide with ordinary React component names: ModelViewer.tsx is
   116	#        not a data model, ContextMenu.tsx is not React context, RoutePlanner.tsx is
   117	#        not a router. Those read as L3/L2 under _kw, and a floor that cries danger
   118	#        at a UI component is a floor people learn to route around — which costs far
   119	#        more than the handful of real files it would have caught.
   120	#        `models/`, `model.ts` and `Schema.ts` still match: only the HUMP form is
   121	#        restricted, because that is the only form the collision takes.
   122	def _kw(words):
   123	    return rf"(?:{words})(?=(?-i:[A-Z])|[/._\-\s]|$)"
   124	
   125	
   126	def _kws(words):
   127	    return rf"(?:(?-i:{words})(?=(?-i:[A-Z]))|(?:{words})(?=[/._\-\s]|$))"
   128	
