  2215	# The words real reviewers actually end on. Sampled from the fleet's captured
  2216	# transcripts: Codex ends "VERDICT: PASS", GLM's red-team scale is
  2217	# "Verdict: PASS / PASS WITH RISKS / REQUEST CHANGES / BLOCK", and some runs
  2218	# emit lowercase "verdict: fail" or a GitHub-style "APPROVE". A parser that
  2219	# only knew uppercase "VERDICT: PASS" scored every one of those a fail —
  2220	# fail-closed, but useless: it would have made `check` unusable with the very
  2221	# reviewers this repo ships. Unknown wording still fails.
  2222	# The reviewer's verdict is a MACHINE PROTOCOL, not prose to be interpreted.
  2223	# Exactly one canonical marker line counts:
  2224	#
  2225	#     VERDICT: PASS        (or)        VERDICT: FAIL
  2226	#
  2227	# The reviewer PROMPT tells the reviewer to end with it; the gate does not try
  2228	# to infer approval from natural language. "PASS WITH RISKS", "APPROVE",
  2229	# "LGTM", "SHIP" and every other human phrasing are NOT a pass — inferring one
  2230	# would let a hedged review land as `pass` with zero open findings. Missing,
  2231	# malformed, truncated, ambiguous or non-canonical: FAIL CLOSED.
  2232	# Case-INSENSITIVE on purpose: a lowercase "verdict: fail" is still a verdict,
  2233	# and it must be seen so it can be rejected as non-canonical rather than skipped.
  2234	_VERDICT_MARKER = re.compile(r"^[ \t]*VERDICT:.*$", re.M | re.I)
