  1268	+## The baseline, and a known cost (deferred: "verification is not authorization")
  1269	+
  1270	+The gate walks back from HEAD to find the last commit whose evidence it can
  1271	+trust, and only classifies the paths that changed *since* that baseline. A
  1272	+report advances the baseline only if it would pass its **own full evaluation** —
  1273	+tier covering its segment, tests green, the reviews its tier requires, and the
  1274	+named human approval where policy requires one.
  1275	+
  1276	+That last condition has a real cost, and it is currently paid on purpose. A
  1277	+fully reviewed L3 that nobody got around to countersigning is treated as if it
  1278	+had never been reviewed: its files fall back into every later change's floor, so
  1279	+a docs-only commit can inherit L3 from the repo root. Measured across nine
  1280	+repos, 238 reviewed-but-unsigned L3 commits were being dropped from baselines
  1281	+this way, which is most of the fleet's inflated floors.
  1282	+
  1283	+**The fix for it is deferred, and the reason is worth recording.** Letting
  1284	+*verified* evidence advance the baseline requires the signature that commit
  1285	+still owes to be carried forward to HEAD — otherwise a docs commit stacked on an
  1286	+unapproved migration gates at L1 and merges the migration with it. Five
  1287	+consecutive cold review rounds each found a different way to shed that
  1288	+obligation: stacking a commit on top, `--human-gate-scope irreversible`, an L0
  1289	+successor, the scope going retroactively sticky, `--base`, and finally carrying
  1290	+the human approval while dropping the reviewer requirement. Every fix created
  1291	+the next hole. The mechanism needs a design, not another condition, so the
  1292	+conservative behaviour stands: **over-classification, which costs review time,
  1293	+rather than a gate that can be walked around.**
